\section{Error Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

To understand \emph{why} different ambiguity types produce different violation rates, we analyze 245 violation traces (123 from GPT-5.4, 122 from GPT-4.1) coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Signatures}

Each ambiguity type produces a distinctive failure profile. Authorization scope violations are dominated by unauthorized escalation (72\% for GPT-5.4, 83\% for GPT-4.1): agents exceed permission boundaries when the scope of ``authorized personnel'' or ``approved actions'' is underspecified. Incompleteness triggers assumption-based action (61\% and 66\%): agents fill information gaps by inventing plausible but incorrect defaults. Scopal ambiguity leads to scope misapplication (76\% and 74\%): agents attach quantifiers, negation, or modals to the wrong constituent. Lexical ambiguity also triggers assumption-based action (71\% and 53\%), as agents select one word sense without considering alternatives. Conditional precedence produces a flatter distribution, split between scope misapplication (41\% and 28\%) and arbitrary rule selection (29\% and 28\%), reflecting the multiple resolution strategies available when explicit rules conflict.

These signatures are cross-model consistent: five of six types share the same dominant failure mode across both annotated models. Only coreferential diverges, with GPT-5.4 favoring scope misapplication (44\%) and GPT-4.1 favoring assumption-based action (38\%). The overall failure mode distribution is nearly identical between models ($\sim$33\% assumption-based action, $\sim$29\% unauthorized escalation, $\sim$27\% scope misapplication).

\subsection{Layer Mechanism}

The failure mode taxonomy reveals distinct layer-level mechanisms. Linguistic-layer types are dominated by scope misapplication (39\% average vs.\ 15\% for specification-layer), representing a ``misparse-and-apply'' pattern: the agent constructs an incorrect parse of the ambiguous clause and follows it consistently. Specification-layer types are dominated by unauthorized escalation and assumption-based action ($\sim$65\% combined), representing a ``guess-and-act'' pattern: the agent recognizes that information is needed but proceeds with a fabricated resolution. The guess-and-act mechanism produces higher violation rates because the agent actively extends its authority, whereas misparse-and-apply errors are bounded by the clause's literal content.

\subsection{Ambiguity Blindness}

Agents rarely recognize that a clause is ambiguous. Across all types, GPT-5.4 shows 4.1\% disambiguation awareness and GPT-4.1 shows 10.7\%. Surface adoption (proceeding with a single interpretation without acknowledgment) exceeds 88\% for every type in both models. Escalation to the user occurs in fewer than 1\% of episodes. The 2.6$\times$ awareness gap between GPT-4.1 and GPT-5.4 suggests that frontier models may be \emph{more} action-oriented, committing to interpretations with greater confidence. We term this phenomenon \emph{Ambiguity Blindness}: agents process grammatically fluent ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness, adopting surface interpretations by default.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among the 300 ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3 of 5 models violate), with 13\% producing universal violations (all 5 models). This convergent-but-wrong pattern extends Berry and Kamsties' binary distinction between nocuous (divergent interpretations) and innocuous (convergent) ambiguity \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}: a third category, \emph{systematic nocuity}, arises when all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation. Systematic nocuity is more dangerous than divergent nocuity because it is invisible in inter-model agreement metrics and would appear ``correct'' without ground-truth comparison. Incompleteness shows the highest universal convergence rate (24\% of clauses trigger all 5 models), consistent with its status as a universal vulnerability.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline with expert review. For each type, we identify policy clauses in $\tau^2$-bench that are naturally susceptible to the target ambiguity, then construct the ambiguous and unambiguous variants. All pairs are reviewed to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target ambiguity type, (2) the unambiguous variant eliminates that ambiguity without introducing new information that changes the policy's scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural (would occur in real policy documents).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning three providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 traces. We acknowledge this creates an asymmetry (a mid-tier model judging a frontier model in one direction), which we discuss in \S\ref{sec:limitations}. Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. For the primary analysis, we binarize this into violation (any non-none outcome) versus no violation.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm that the observed effect is specific to semantic ambiguity rather than general prompt sensitivity, we run 120 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations: synonym substitution (replacing content words with near-synonyms) and clause reordering (shuffling the order of policy sub-clauses). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (95\% CI $[-5.0, 13.3]$, containing zero), compared to the ambiguity effect of $\Delta_{\text{ambig}} = 34.0$ pp.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} To verify that violation rates are stable across independent runs, we replicate 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates. The intraclass correlation coefficient is ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), indicating excellent reproducibility.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes (ambiguous vs.\ unambiguous within each clause pair), $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus type and model effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, TOST (two one-sided tests) equivalence testing with $\pm 15$ pp and $\pm 10$ pp margins, bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples), and logistic regression with condition, type, model, and token delta as predictors. All tests use $\alpha = 0.05$ with Holm-Bonferroni correction where multiple comparisons are made.

\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

We adopt an operational definition of ambiguity following \citet{wasow_ambiguity_2005}: a policy clause is ambiguous when it admits multiple distinct interpretations available to a competent reader, including cases of vagueness where the boundary between interpretations is gradient rather than discrete \citep{kennedy_vagueness_2007}.

Our taxonomy comprises six types organized into two layers, drawing on \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024}'s NLP taxonomy (11 types), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014}'s requirements engineering taxonomy (6 types), and the concurrent DRIP-R benchmark \citep{drip_r_2026} (5 types). We select types based on two criteria: frequency in operational policy documents (assessed via manual audit of $\tau^2$-bench policies) and theoretical distinctiveness (each type maps to a different resolution mechanism).

\paragraph{Linguistic layer.} These types operate at the syntactic or semantic level and can, in principle, be resolved by parsing or contextual cues within the text.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Scopal}: quantifier, negation, or modal scope interactions (e.g., ``all items may not be returned'' admits universal-negation and negation-of-universal readings).
\item \textbf{Lexical}: polysemy or homonymy (e.g., ``charge'' as financial cost vs.\ penalty; ``party'' as individual vs.\ group).
\item \textbf{Coreferential}: unclear pronoun or noun-phrase reference (e.g., ``the agent should verify the customer's identity before processing their refund request, which must be approved by a supervisor'' where ``which'' could refer to the verification or the refund).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Specification layer.} These types arise from pragmatic or discourse-level gaps that require domain knowledge or organizational context to resolve.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Authorization scope}: vague permission or role boundaries (e.g., ``authorized personnel may issue refunds'' without specifying which personnel or under what conditions).
\item \textbf{Incompleteness}: missing conditions, exceptions, or boundary cases (e.g., a return policy that specifies the return window but not what happens when the item is damaged).
\item \textbf{Conditional precedence}: conflicting or overlapping rules without priority ordering (e.g., ``premium customers receive free shipping'' and ``orders under \$50 require a shipping fee'' without specifying which rule takes precedence for a premium customer with a \$30 order).
\end{itemize}

The layer distinction reflects the hypothesis that linguistic ambiguity can be partially resolved through tool responses, user context, or world knowledge, while specification ambiguity has no external resolution channel available to the agent, forcing it to guess. We test this hypothesis empirically in \S\ref{sec:results} and \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that human comprehenders build shallow, ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, a finding that parallels our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations of ambiguous policy clauses. \citet{frazier_rayner_1982} demonstrate that readers commit to a single syntactic parse early and backtrack upon disambiguation failure, while \citet{levy_expectation_2008} formalizes this as expectation-based processing. \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} provide direct evidence that LLMs exhibit human-like garden-path effects, and \citet{poesio_artstein_2005} formalize genuine ambiguity in anaphoric annotation as inherent rather than resolvable. \citet{lassiter_goodman_2017} model vague adjective interpretation in a Bayesian framework. These findings ground our ``Ambiguity Blindness'' result: agents, like humans, process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness, but unlike humans, they lack the social and organizational channels to recover.

\paragraph{Agent safety evaluation.}
The agent safety evaluation landscape includes benchmarks for risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, indirect prompt injection \citep{injecagent_2024}, and policy-invisible violations \citep{phantompolicy_2026}. \citet{shieldagent_2025} extract formal safety rules from policy documents, and AgentAuditor \citep{agentauditor_2025} achieves human-level safety evaluation. Our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than the agent's execution: we measure how ambiguity in the policy itself causes safety violations, complementing work that assumes clean policies.

\paragraph{Policy specification for agents.}
DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work: it benchmarks 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types, measuring decision quality (answer correctness) rather than safety violations. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement, closing 82\% of the gap to a human oracle. AgentPex \citep{agentpex_2026} detects procedural violations in agentic traces, finding that 83\% of perfect-outcome traces contain violations. MANTRA \citep{mantra_2026} synthesizes SMT-validated compliance benchmarks from procedural manuals. $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and its successor $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide the conversational agent evaluation platforms. We build on this foundation by providing the first causal, type-granularity, cross-model mapping from policy ambiguity to safety violations.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

