\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers (linguistic and specification, \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal \emph{Differential Nocuity}: all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, OR $= 15.1$), but the type of ambiguity and the model jointly determine the severity. Incompleteness is universally dangerous (46--62\% violation rate across all models), while authorization scope vulnerability varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4), suggesting that safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness across all types, adopting surface interpretations in over 88\% of cases.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The first causal quantification of policy ambiguity's safety impact, using matched-pair design with perturbation control ($\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp vs.\ $\Delta_{\text{ambig}} = 34.0$ pp) and cross-architecture replication across five models (\S\ref{sec:results}).
\item A Differential Nocuity framework showing a significant three-tier type hierarchy ($V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$) and a model$\times$type interaction where authorization scope serves as a ``canary type'' for safety training quality (\S\ref{sec:c4}).
\item A type-specific failure mode taxonomy with empirical evidence for Ambiguity Blindness ($<$11\% awareness, $>$88\% surface adoption) and the mechanistic distinction between linguistic-layer misparse and specification-layer forced guessing (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential Nocuity.}
\citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish nocuous from innocuous ambiguity based on whether stakeholders diverge on interpretation. Our results extend this framework to LLM agents: all six ambiguity types are nocuous ($+34.0$ pp overall), but both the type and the model determine severity. We propose \emph{Differential Nocuity} as the governing principle: the risk of a policy ambiguity is not an intrinsic property of the clause but a function of the ambiguity type, the agent's safety training, and the availability of external disambiguation channels.

\paragraph{Safety training selectivity.}
The model$\times$type interaction (C4) reveals that safety training does not uniformly reduce ambiguity vulnerability. Claude's authorization scope rate (12\%) is 52 pp below GPT-5.4's (64\%), suggesting that Anthropic's alignment process specifically addresses authorization boundary reasoning. In contrast, incompleteness remains universally dangerous (46--62\%) regardless of model or training approach, indicating a ``safety training blind spot'' where no current alignment method provides protection against missing information. Authorization scope thus functions as a \emph{canary type}: a model's vulnerability to authorization scope ambiguity signals the quality of its safety training for permission-related reasoning.

\paragraph{Disambiguation channel framework.}
The layer hierarchy reflects the availability of external disambiguation channels. Linguistic ambiguity (scopal, lexical, coreferential) can sometimes be partially resolved through tool responses, user context, or world knowledge. The higher ICC reliability of linguistic-layer types (0.976--0.989 vs.\ 0.807--0.857) and the prevalence of scope-misapplication failures (misparse rather than guess) support this interpretation. Specification-layer ambiguity, by contrast, concerns information that is absent from any available source, forcing agents to fabricate resolutions. This framework is observational, supported by convergent evidence from ICC patterns, failure mode distributions, and the coreferential boundary case (conservative refusal rate 6--11\%), but has not been tested via direct manipulation. A future experiment providing specification-layer disambiguation information through tool responses could test whether the layer gap diminishes.

\paragraph{Systematic Nocuity.}
Our cross-model analysis reveals that 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations. This extends the nocuous/innocuous binary: in \emph{systematic nocuity}, all models converge on the same wrong interpretation, making the error invisible in agreement-based metrics. This is arguably more dangerous than divergent nocuity (where disagreement at least signals uncertainty) and warrants attention in safety evaluation pipelines that rely on cross-model consensus.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Our findings suggest two design recommendations. First, policy auditing should be \emph{type-aware}: incompleteness and authorization scope ambiguities should be prioritized for disambiguation, as they produce the highest and most model-dependent violation rates. Second, ambiguity detection must be \emph{externalized}: with fewer than 11\% self-awareness across all types, agents cannot be relied upon to flag ambiguous clauses themselves. External detection tools, drawing on the RE ambiguity detection literature \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019}, offer a more reliable triage mechanism.

\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity, yet the causal relationship between ambiguity type and safety violations remains unmeasured. We present a matched-pair study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluated across five architecturally diverse models (2,997 episodes). Ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 percentage points (OR $= 15.1$), with a perturbation control confirming ambiguity-specific causation. A three-tier type hierarchy emerges (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$): incompleteness is universally dangerous (46--62\% across models), while authorization scope varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4), revealing that safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness, with over 88\% surface adoption. Our Differential Nocuity framework connects type-specific vulnerability to disambiguation channel availability and suggests both type-aware policy auditing and externalized ambiguity detection for deployed systems.
\end{abstract}

